[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m In the complex plane, the line segment with end-points $-11 + 3i$ and $3 - 7i$ is plotted in the complex plane.  Find the complex number corresponding to the mid-point of this line segment.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m ième
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To find the mid-point of a line segment in the complex plane, we first need to find the complex coordinates of the two given end-points: $-11 + 3i$ and $3 - 7i$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We can add these two complex numbers together to get their sum:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $-11 + 3i + 3 - 7i = (-11 + 3) + (3 + 7)i$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $= -8 + 10i$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So, the mid-point of the line segment is $-8 + 10i$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To find the complex number corresponding to the mid-point, we can choose any globally real $z$, and then multiply it by $2/(z^{-1} + z + z^{-1})$. Since $z$ is globally real, this formula gives us the complex number corresponding to the mid-point of the line segment:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $2/(z^{-1} + z + z^{-1}) z$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $= 2/(2z - 1) z$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $= (2z - 1)/(z)$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $= 2 - \frac{z}{z}$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So, the complex number corresponding to the mid-point of the line segment is $2 - \frac{1}{z}$.<|endoftext|>
