[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $2^{x-3}=4^{x+1}$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m First, we know that $4^{x+1} = (2^2)^{x+1} = 2^{2(x+1)} = 2^{2x+2}$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So we can rewrite the equation as:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $2^{x-3}=2^{2x+2}$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Since the bases are the same, we can set the exponents equal to each other:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $x-3=2x+2$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Now, we isolate $x$:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $x-2x=3+2$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $-x=5$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $x=-5$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
