[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Recall the trigonometric identities, such as the sum theorem, multiplication theorem, and double angle formula.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Aussie thanks you
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m iangle components given by $\cos 90^\circ = 0$ and $\sin 90^\circ = 1$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Therefore, to express $\sin 604^\circ$ in terms of $\cos$ and $\sin$ components,
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$604^\circ = 4 \times 156^\circ + 4^\circ.$$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Hence, given that, we obtain
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$\sin 604^\circ = \sin (4 \times 156^\circ + 4^\circ).$$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Using the sum theorem in the form of the cofunction form,
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$\sin (4 \times 156^\circ + 4^\circ) = \cos 4^\circ.$$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Thus, we have $n = 4$ and so the answer is \boxed{4}. navigationOptions
