[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m If each 1/2 inch on a map represents 20 miles, how many inches apart on the map are two cities whose actual distance apart is 120 miles?
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m First, we need to find the distance on the map in miles. We can do this by dividing the actual distance by the map resolution.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \text{Map distance} = \frac{120 \text{ miles}}{2} = 60 \text{ miles}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Next, we need to convert the distance on the map into inches. Since each 1/2 inch represents 20 miles, we can first find the length of one inch in miles.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 1 \text{ inch} = 20 \text{ miles}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Now, we can find the distance on the map in inches. Since there are two cities, we multiply the distance on the map in miles by 2.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \text{Distance on map in inches} = 60 \text{ miles} \times 2 = 120 \text{ miles}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The problem asks how many inches apart the cities are on the map with 120 miles between them. Since the distance on the map in inches is 120, the cities are \boxed{120} inches apart on the map.
