[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Let $\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}.$  Find the vector $\mathbf{b}$ such that $\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b} = 11$ and
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} -13 \\ -9 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We are asked to find the vector $\mathbf{b}$ that satisfies two conditions:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 1) $\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b} = 11$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 2) $\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} -13 \\ -9 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Let's start by solving the first condition: $\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b} = 11$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m This can be expressed as a dot product equation: $\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b} = 2 \cdot b_1 + 1 \cdot b_2 + 5 \cdot b_3 = 11.$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m This gives us a system of linear equations:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 2b_1 + b_2 + 5b_3 = 11 & \text{(Equation 1)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m b_1 + 2b_2 + 5b_3 = 11 & \text{(Equation 2)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m b_1 + b_2 + 2b_3 = 11 & \text{(Equation 3)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \end{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Our goal is to find a vector $\mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \\ b_3 \end{pmatrix}.$ We can solve this system using Gaussian elimination or any other method. In this case, we'll solve it as a system in matrix form.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & 5 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \\ b_3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 11 \\ 11 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix}$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m This can be expressed in matrix form as:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & 5 \\ 1 & 2 & 5 \\ 5 & 5 & 2 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \\ b_3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 11 \\ 11 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We can solve by multiplying the bottom row by half of the top row to get a pivot matrix. Solving:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 & 3 \\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\ 5 & 3 & -9 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \\ b_3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 11 \\ 11 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m This results in:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 & 3 \\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\ 5 & 3 & -9 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \\ b_3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We will rowreduce this matrix:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 & 3 \\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\ 5 & 3 & -9 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 & 3 \\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\ 0 & 2 & -18 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The matrix is now in row-reduced echelon form. The pivot columns are the columns that contain a leading 1. Let's proceed to eliminate this. We'll multiply the second row by -3 and add it to the third row:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 & 3 \\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\ 0 & 2 & -18 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & -4 \\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\ 0 & 2 & -18 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Now, we'll eliminate the second element in the first and third rows:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & -4 \\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\ 0 & 0 & 6 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & -4 \\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\ 0 & 0 & 2 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m This gives us:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & -4 \\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\ 0 & 2 & 0 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We can solve this system:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - From the third pivot equation: $\mathbf{b}_3 = 0.$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - So we have $b_3 = 0.$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - Now we substitute back into the second equation to find $b_2$: $1 + 2b_2 + 0 = 11$ gives $2b_2 = 10$ or $b_2 = 5.$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - Finally we substitute back into the first equation: $2b_1 + 5 + 0 = 11$ gives $2b_1 = 6$ or $b_1 = 3.$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So the vector $\mathbf{b}$ is $\mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 5 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}.$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m boxed{ \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 5 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}}<|endoftext|>
