[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The function $f(x)$ satisfies
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[xf(y) = yf(x)\]for all real numbers $x$ and $y.$  If $f(15) = 20,$ find $f(3).$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We have been given that $xf(y) = yf(x)\;\;\;\;(*)\;\;\;.$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Since $f\_1(15) = 20,$ we can use this equation to find the general solution for $f\_n(15)$ in terms of $n$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m For $n = 1,$ we can plug in $x = 15$ into the equation:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[15 \cdot y = y f(15) \implies f(15) = y \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So we have that $f\_1(15) = 20,$ which means that $15 \cdot y = y \cdot 20,$ which simplifies to $x = 20.$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m In general, we can use the equation $(*)$ to find $f\_{n+1}(x),$ which is $xf\_n(x).$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So for $n = 1$ we have $f\_1(x) = 15 \cdot f\_1(1) = 15 \cdot 20 = 300.$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m For $n = 2$ we have $f\_{2}(x) = x \cdot f\_1(x) = \boxed{300x}.$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m In general, we can see that $f\_{n+1}(x) = \boxed{x \cdot 20^{n+1}}.$
