[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m If $T$ is a relation from names to names, then $R$ is a relation if and only if $T \circ R = T$. This shows that for any relation, there is a relation.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Let's denote a relation by $R$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Recall, a relation from names to names is a proper subset or inclusion of
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m names, $\Pi_{R}$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Conversely, a relation is a proper subset or inclusion if and only if $\Pi_R$ is a relation.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We say $\varphi$ is an "inverse direction" or "inclusion" if and only if $\varphi
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \circ \varphi = \varphi$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Our premise is, however, that it is trivial that $\Pi_{R}$ is a proper subset or inclusion
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m of $\varphi \circ \varphi \setminus \varphi$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m That is,
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m it is very trivial for a relation $\varphi$ to be a proper subset or inclusion
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m if and only if $\varphi$ is the inverse of $\Pi_{R}$.
