[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m What is the greatest common factor of all two-digit palindromes?  (Note: A palindrome is a number that reads the same forwards as backwards.)
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To find the greatest common factor (GCF) of all two-digit palindromes, we first identify all the two-digit palindromes.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 5 & \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m + & \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 7 & \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m + & \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 9 \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m In ascending order, the two-digit palindromes are: \( 55, 77, 99 \).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Next, we find the prime factors of each two-digit palindrome:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - \( 5 \) (since there are no two-digit numbers ending with an odd digit) has 1 and itself.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - \( 7 \) (prime) has 7.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - \( 9 \) (prime) has 9.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The common prime factors among \( 5 \), \( 7 \), and \( 9 \) are 1 and 7 (since both 7 and 1 have the same prime factors).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Thus, the greatest common factor of all two-digit palindromes is:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \(\boxed{7}\)<|endoftext|>
