[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 1. **Understanding Grid Dimensions**: The grid is an \( n \times m \) grid, where \( n \) is the number of steps from \( A \) to \( B \). If \( n = k \), it means \( A \) is at the (0,0) position and \( B \) is at the (k-1,k-1) position.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 2. **Path Constraints**: Since we can only move right or up, any path from \( A \) to \( B \) must consist of exactly \( k-1 \) steps to the right (R) and \( k-1 \) steps up (U).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 3. **Counting the Paths**:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m     - The first step of the path must be to the right (R). There is only one unique way to do this.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m     - The second step of the path must be up (U). There is only one unique way to do this.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m     - This pattern continues until the total number of steps taken (k-1) is used up.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Since each step is uniquely determined by the direction (down or right), and the number of such paths is simply one, the number of paths from \( A \) to \( B \) is \( 1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
