[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We are given that the area is $21$ square inches and the original square's side $(a)$ is $2$ inches.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Therefore, we can calculate the original area as $a\times a=2a^2=21$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Dividing both sides by $2$ gives us $a^2=10.5$, and $\pm\sqrt{10.5}=4.5-1.5\pm\sqrt{10.5}\approx 2.6\ \text{inches}$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Now, if we want to increase $2.6$ inches to get the side length of the new square, the side area would be $2.6^2=16.96$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Since the area of the new square is greater than the original one, it produces two possibilities. If the new square has a longer side, $\sqrt{16.96}=4.1\ [in \ ]$ What if the new square has a shorter side. $\sqrt{16.96}=2.9\ [in \ ]$
