[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To simplify the given expression, I will use the fact that $a,b,c$ are nonzero constants.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m First, let's notice that both terms have the factor $\frac{1}{a}$, so I will take that out of the fraction. I get:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$\frac{3a^2b}{5ac}\times\frac{10c}{6ab}=\frac{1}{a}\times\frac{10c}{6bc}\times\frac{2b}{3a}$$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Next, I can use the fact that the $3a$'s cancel and the $5a$'s cancel with the $6ab$'s and $6bc$'s, leaving me with just:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$\frac{1}{a}\times\frac{10c}{2b\cdot3c}$$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$\frac{1}{a}\times\frac{10c}{6bc}$$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Now I know that $b$ and $c$ are nonzero constants, so my numerator can be reduced down a bit. $10c/b$ becomes $10/c$ because $10/c = 10/b$, and $6c/b$ becomes $6/c$ because $6/b = 6/c$. Keeping this in mind, the simplified expression is:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$\frac{1}{b}\times\frac{10}{c}$$
