[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Start with your equation: $1 + 2x + 3x^2 + x \cdots + ax^{k-1}+ ... + dx^{10}$ Substitute $x=1$ on both sides (exponent being $0$ by definition of the sum of terms). And obtain expressions for $2a,3a,y,3y, \cdots,10y$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m leDbreak
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Consider the family of lines \begin{align\*}Horizontal\, Line\, and\, Vertical\, Lines.\end{align\* Both families seems to be through constant $z$, but the y and z scale are becoming separate:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m ![enter image description here](https://i.stack.imgur.com/c7cDy.png)
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m  Upon getting your expression for $z$ and comparing it to that of $1$ we get the following constants:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m ![enter image description here](https://i.stack.imgur.com/5fRA3.png)
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So, as you go from the left to the right (vertical axis orientation), by induction, we conclude that:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$ \begin{aligned}(1)_0&=- a_0 \\ (1)_1&=2a_0- a_1 \\ (1)_2&=3a_0+ 3a_1- a_2 \\ \cdots&=(k)_1+k\left[2a_0- \left[k-1\right]a_1-  {a_{k-1}}\right]-a_k\end{aligned}$$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m However, it seems that an easier approach is to use the fact that
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$\begin{aligned}(1)_k&=k \cdot \sum_{j=0}^{k-1} \left(2a_0- \left(j+1\right) a_1\right)-a_k \\ &=k \left[2a_0- \left(\frac{k-1}{k+1} k a_0- \left(\frac{k-1}{k+1} k \cdot 1\right) a_{k-1}\right)\right]-a_k \\ &=1 \cdot \sum_{j=0}^{k-1} \left(2a_0- \left(j+1\right) a_1\right)-a_k \\ &=1 \cdot \sum_{j=0}^{k-1} \left(2a_0+ja_1+1\cdot (j+1) a_1\right)-a_{k}\end{aligned}$$<|endoftext|>
