[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m If the numbers on the third diagonal of Pascal's Triangle are triangular numbers, what is the value of the $50$th triangular number? (The $n$th triangular number is $1+2+3+\ldots+n$.)
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To solve this, we need to compute the fifth row of Pascal's Triangle and then count the triangular numbers in it.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \(\displaystyle\,\begin{array}{|ccc|ccc|}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \hline
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & \cdots \\ \hline
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6 & \cdots \\ \hline
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m & 1 & 3 & 6 & 10 & 15 & 21 & \cdots \\ \
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Now, looking at the \(50\)th column in Pascal's Triangle, we see that there are \(49\) ones.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Thus, the triangular number in the \(50\)th row of Pascal's Triangle is \(\boxed{49*49} = \boxed{2401}\).
