[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m How many 5-digit numbers have at least one 2 or one 3 among their digits?
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Alright let's solve this problem step by step!
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To solve this problem, we have to think about 5-digit numbers. Since any of the 10 digits (0-9) can be in our 5-digit number, we will have:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m  × 10 × 10 × 10 × 10 × 10 = 10^5
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m This is because the 5: 2 and 3 bytes can be the first, fourth, fifth, and sixth digit respectively:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 1. 2 in 2 3
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 2. 3 in 2 3
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 3. 2 in 1 3
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 4. 1 in 1 3
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 5. 3 in 1 3
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So there are 10^5 possible 5-digit numbers, which equals `10,000,000`.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
