[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Find the distance from the point $(1,-1,2)$ to the line passing through $(-2,2,1)$ and $(-1,-1,3).$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m  A line passing through the points $(-2,2,1)$ and $(-1,-1,3)$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m By analytical method:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m  The line is the one that passes through $(-2,2,1)$ and $(-1,-1,3)$ and relates to the points specified.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Here, $(-2,2,1)$ and $(-1,-1,3)$ are the endpts of the line.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Compute the midpoint between the two points: \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m  M_{mid} &= \left(\frac{-2 + (-1)}{2},\frac{(2 + (-1))}{2},\frac{(1 + 3)}{2}\right) \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m  &= \left(-\frac{3}{2},\frac{1}{2},2\right)
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Compute the vector form of the line passing through $(-2,2,1)$ and $(-1,-1,3)$ as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$ \vec{r}(t) = \left([\frac{1}{2} \times t] \vec{a} + [2 t -1] \vec{b} + [1 -t] \vec{c}\right)$$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$ &= \frac{1}{2} \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 2 \\ 1 \\ \end{pmatrix} + \frac{1}{2} \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ -1 \\ 3 \\ \end{pmatrix} + \left(1 - t \right) \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 1 \\ 2 \\ \end{pmatrix}$$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Compute the vector distance from $(-1,-1,3)$ to $M_{mid}$(which is not on the line) as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$ d(M_{mid}) = \left\| \left[\frac{1}{2} \times (-1) + \frac{1}{2} \times 2 + \left(1 - t \right) \begin{pmatrix} -3 \right] \right]\right\|$$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m By using Pluecker equation:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$ \frac{1}{2} (\| \mathbf{a} \| \| \mathbf{b} \| - d_{\mathbf{a}} d_{\mathbf{b}}) \| \mathbf{c} \| $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Compute the distance from the point $(1,-1,2)$ to the line passing through $(-2,2,1)$ and $(-1,-1,3)$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The Pluquer formula is correct as to the computation of the (pseudo) distance from P(1, -1, 2) to the line. \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m  d_M &= d_{\mathbf p}\\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m  &= \frac{
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \left[\frac{1}{2} (\vec{a}) \cdot (\vec{b}) - d_{\mathbf \vec{a}} d_{\mathbf \vec{b}} \right]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m }
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m {\frac{1}{2} (\| \vec{a} \| \| \vec{b} \| - d_{\mathbf \vec{a}} d_{\mathbf \vec{b}} \notag}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m = 1} -1
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \end{align*}<|endoftext|>
