[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m If $f(x)= \frac{x+2}{2-x}$, then $f(x)$ is defined for ______. To determine the domain of the function \( f(x) = \frac{x+2}{2-x} \), we need to identify the values of \( x \) for which the function is defined. The function is undefined when the denominator is zero, so we need to solve for \( x \) in the equation \( 2 - x = 0 \).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Here are the steps to find the domain:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 1. Set the denominator equal to zero: \( 2 - x = 0 \).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 2. Solve for \( x \):
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    2 - x = 0 \implies x = 2
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    However, we must check if this value is in the domain of the original function \( f(x) \). The original function is defined for all real numbers except where the denominator is zero, i.e., \( 2 - x \neq 0 \), which means \( x \neq 2 \).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Since \( x = 2 \) is not in the domain, the only value that makes the function undefined is \( x = 2 \). Therefore, the domain of \( f(x) \) is all real numbers except \( x = 2 \).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m In interval notation, the domain is \( (-\infty, 2) \cup (2, \infty) \).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Thus, the domain of \( f(x) \) is \(\boxed{(-\infty, 2) \cup (2, \infty)}\).<|endoftext|>
