[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To determine how many tiles are needed to cover a three-foot by two-foot rectangular section of floor, let's break down the problem step by step:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 1. **Convert all measurements to inches:**
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - Three feet = 36 inches
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - Two feet = 24 inches
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - Floor dimensions = 3 feet by 2 feet
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m After conversion:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - Floor area = \(3 \text{ feet} \times 36 \text{ inches/foot} = 108 \text{ square inches}\)
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - Two-foot tile dimensions = 6 inches by 6 inches
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 2. **Calculate the total number of tiles:**
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - Since each tile is 6 inches by 6 inches, and we need a square tile, the area of each tile rows is \(6 \text{ inches} \times 6 \text{ inches} = 36 \text{ square inches}\).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We will divide the floor area by the tile area:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - Number of tiles horizontally = \( \frac{108 \text{ square inches}}{36 \text{ square inches}} = 3 \) tiles 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - Number of tiles vertically = \( \frac{108 \text{ square inches}}{36 \text{ square inches}} = 3 \) tiles
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So, we need exactly 9 tiles. 
