[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m If $f(x)$ is a polynomial of degree 3, and $g(x)$ is a polynomial of degree 5, then what is the degree of polynomial $2f(x) + 4g(x)$?
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To determine the degree of the polynomial \( 2f(x) + 4g(x) \), we start by analyzing each term separately.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 1. \( 2f(x) \):
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    - The degree of \( f(x) \) is 3.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    - When we multiply a degree 3 polynomial by 2, the degree of the resulting polynomial depends on how the powers of \( x \) in \( f(x) \) scale when multiplied by 2. Since \( f(x) \) has a degree of 3, it will introduce \( x^3 \) term in \( 2f(x) \).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    - When \( f(x) \) introduces \( x^3 \) term, applying the multiplication to \( 2f(x) \) results in \( 2x^3 \) term.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 2. \( 4g(x) \):
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    - The degree of \( g(x) \) is 5.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    - When we multiply a degree 5 polynomial by 4, the degree of the resulting polynomial depends on how the powers of \( x \) in \( g(x) \) scale when multiplied by 4. Since \( g(x) \) has a degree of 5, it will introduce \( x^5 \) term in \( 4g(x) \).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    - When \( g(x) \) introduces \( x^5 \) term, applying the multiplication to \( 4g(x) \) results in \( 4x^5 \) term.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 3. Combining the terms:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    - The combined term \( 2f(x) + 4g(x) \) introduces \( x^3 \), \( x^5 \), and a coefficient of 2 for the \( x^5 \) term.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - The total degree of the polynomial \( 2f(x) + 4g(x) \) is the maximum of the degrees of its individual terms. This is because polynomials with terms of identical degrees follow the order of operations, where the term with the higher degree multiplies as much as possible.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Thus, the highest degree term among the three is \( x^5 \), which is the term with a greater degree.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Therefore, the degree of the polynomial \( 2f(x) + 4g(x) \) is \(\boxed{5}\).<|endoftext|>
