[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The $\text{gcd}(a,b)$ is $\min \lbrace a,b \rbrace$. Your solution for $\cos x$ seems correct. However, where is your insight into why $\sin r\theta +\cos r \theta = 1$? Splice a part of geometry that was previously skipped, anyway: $\sin^2\theta + \cos^2\theta = 1$. But the problem says nothing about $r$. Perhaps I was a bit hasty in the vocabulary, so note: $r$ does not mean the radius of a circle as it would in so-called geometric algebra. It is actually another generalization of complex numbers. More precisely, $\left|\;r\;\right| = \sqrt{r^T r} = \sqrt{r^2}$, as the absolute value of a number is the length of its real root, here the length of the square root of the length of the vector.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Similarily, $\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta = 1$, as the square of a number is the sum of the square roots of its quadratic. It is well-known that cos^2θ + sin^2θ can also imply unity, $\cos^2 \theta = 1$, which also can be proven through geometry. However, in the future one can do the actual calculation $\cos \theta \cdot \sin \theta$ using the Pythagorean Theorem: $\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta = 1$, which would lead to the same conclusion.<|endoftext|>
