[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m It looks more complicated, but if you do not confuse the numbers of these matrices, you will notice that everything is almost the same within the transpose. You can remove the parentheses. All you have to do is
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m & \begin{bmatrix} \boxed{x+y-1.5} & \boxed{y-z} \\ \boxed{w+z} & \boxed{y} \end{bmatrix} & \begin{bmatrix} \boxed{x} & \boxed{z+y} \\ \boxed{w} & \boxed{y} \end{bmatrix} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m & \begin{bmatrix} \boxed{x & z} & \boxed{y} \\ \boxed{y+z} & \boxed{w} \end{bmatrix} & \begin{bmatrix} \boxed{x+y-1} & \boxed{y-z} \\ \boxed{w} & \boxed{z} \end{bmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The product of the two matrices, with $x$ and $z$ located in the first row and $y$ in the second row, looks like
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m & \begin{bmatrix} \boxed{x+y-1.5} & \boxed{y-z} \\ \boxed{w+z} & \boxed{y} \end{bmatrix} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m & \begin{bmatrix} \boxed{x} & \boxed{z} \\ \boxed{y+z} & \boxed{w} \end{bmatrix} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m & \begin{bmatrix} \boxed{x+y+1} & \boxed{y-z} \\ \boxed{w+z} & \boxed{y} \end{bmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We perform the column multiplication: the first column of the second matrix multiplied by the first column of the first matrix equals, in the first location, \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m [x+y+1] \times [x+y-1.5] & =\boxed{\boxed{1} * \boxed{1} =\boxed{1}\\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m [x+y+1] \times [y-z] & =\boxed{\boxed{1} * \boxed{1} =\boxed{1}\\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m [x+y+1] \times [w+z] & =\boxed{\boxed{1} * \boxed{1} =\boxed{1}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m and finally the remaining two columns of the first matrix are multiplied. each column filled with two numbers \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m     [[1] [\boxed{1}] [\boxed{1}]} & =\boxed{\boxed{\boxed{1}}}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m and
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m     [[1] [\boxed{1}] [\boxed{1}]} & =\boxed{\boxed{\boxed{1}}}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Now, you can simply complete these rows of the first matrix with the rest of the entries in the tail.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m & \begin{bmatrix} \boxed{x+y-1.5} & \boxed{y-z} \\ \boxed{w+z} & \boxed{y} \end{bmatrix} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m & \begin{bmatrix} \boxed{x} & \boxed{z} \\ \boxed{y+z} & \boxed{w} \end{bmatrix} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m & \begin{bmatrix} \boxed{x+y+1} & \boxed{y-z} \\ \boxed{w+z} & \boxed{y} \end{bmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m * Alternatively, we can increase the size of the matrix by the third row. We can transposition the matrix and use some common matrices $\begin{bmatrix} \boxed{x-z} & \boxed{x+y-z} \\ \boxed{y-z} & \boxed{wz} \end{bmatrix}$. After the transpose, it will look like
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{align*}
