[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Each of Natalie's blueberry bushes yields eight containers of blueberries. If she can trade five containers of blueberries for two zucchinis, how many bushes does Natalie need to pick in order to have forty-eight zucchinis?
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To solve this problem, we need to determine how many bushels of blueberries and how many zucchinis Natalie can generate through her plantations without dipping into the bulk storage of produce.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We start with the information that each bush yields 8 containers of blueberries and that 5 containers can be traded for 2 zucchinis. 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m First, we calculate the net yield of blueberries per bush through 5 trades:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \text{Net yield of blueberries per bush} = 8 \text{ containers} - 2 \text{ zucchinis per trade} = 6 \text{ containers per trade} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Next, we estimate how many trades would be required to yield 5 containers of blueberries:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \text{Number of trades needed to yield 5 containers} = \frac{5 \text{ containers}}{6 \text{ containers per trade}} \approx 0.833 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Since the individual bush cannot have a fraction of a bush, we round up to 1 full trade (since each full trade must yield at least 2 containers):
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \text{Number of bushels required for 5 containers} = 1 \times 6 \text{ containers per trade} = 6 \text{ bushels} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Then, we need to determine how many zucchinis can be produced from these 6 bushels. We consider that 5 containers can be traded for 2 zucchinis:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \text{Number of zucchinis produced per bushel} = \frac{2 \text{ zucchinis per trade}}{5 \text{ containers}} \approx 0.4 \text{ zucchinis per bushel} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So, we calculate the total number of zucchinis that 6 bushes can produce:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \text{Total number of zucchinis} = 6 \text{ bushels} \times 0.4 \text{ zucchinis per bushel} = 2.4 \text{ zucchinis} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Finally, we calculate the actual number of bushes Natalie requires if she has 48 zucchinis available. 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Since 2.4 zucchinis is equivalent to 1 bush:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \text{Number of bushes required} = \frac{1}{2.4} \text{ bushmen} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Recognizing that \(\frac{1}{2.4} = 0.41667\) follows through to round up to 1 bush:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \text{Number of bushes required} = \boxed{1} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Thus, Natalie needs to pick 1 bush of blueberries to have forty-eight zucchinis available.<|endoftext|>
