[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Let \( y = \log_2 (27 + x) \). Then the expression can be written as:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ y = \log_2 (27 + y). \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m This implies:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ 2^y = 27 + y, \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m or equivalently:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ y^2 - y - 27 = 0. \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We now solve this quadratic equation for \( y \):
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ (y - 9)(y + 3) = 0. \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Thus, the solutions are \( y = 9 \) and \( y = -3 \). Both solutions are valid because they give different logarithmic expressions for \( \log_2(27) \).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Now substituting these values back into the original expression, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m When \( y = 9 \):
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \log_2 (27 + x) = 9 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ 2^9 = 27 + x \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ 512 = 27 + x \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ x = 512 - 27 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ x = 485 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \text{Solutions: } 485 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m When \( y = -3 \):
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \log_2 (27 - x) = -3 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Taking the base 2 logarithm on both sides, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \log_2 (27 - x) = -3 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ 2^{-3} = 27 - x \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \frac{1}{8} = 27 - x \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ x = 27 - \frac{1}{8} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ x = \frac{217}{8} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \text{Solutions: } \frac{217}{8}, 485 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m However, we need to check the problem conditions as if there is a base typo. Given the problem statement, we might expect the base to be clearly implied or known from context. In this case, we will consider the base 2 values' solutions to \( \log_2 x \) since the problem might imply a common interpretation of \( 27 \) as a base 2 expression within the scope of straightforward examimations.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So, assuming \( \log_2 (27) = 2 \):
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \log_2 (27 + x) = 2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ 2^2 = 27 + x \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ 4 = 27 + x \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ x = 4 - 27 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ x = -23 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \text{Solutions: } -23 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m In conclusion, the value of the expression, considering possible misunderstandings or a base typo, could be:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \boxed{-23} \quad (\text{or } 485\text{ depending on the base interpretation})
