[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 1. First, determine the radius of the hemisphere. Since the area of the base is $100\pi$, we can identify it as the area of a circle with radius $r$. Thus,
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    \pi r^2 = 100\pi
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 2. Simplify and divide to find $r$. The equation simplifies to $r^2 = 100$, so $r = \sqrt 100 = 10$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 3. Now, calculate the total surface area of the hemisphere. An hemisphere consists of the two base circles and an inscribed sphere. 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    - The surface area of the base is the area of the circle with radius 10,
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    100\pi
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    - The surface area of the sphere is the same as that of a sphere, $4\pi r^2$, in this case, giving us,
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    4\pi \cdot 10^2 = 400\pi
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    - Adding both areas gives the total surface area:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    100\pi + 400\pi = 500\pi
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    So, the total surface area of the hemisphere in terms of $\pi$ is 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    \boxed{500\pi}
