[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We will first identify all segments of the normal pentagon, each with a length that will be placed into the table $S=\{\ell, \ell, \ell, p, r, P\}$, and each side of the pentagon is 5, the length of the diagonal of the pentagon is 8. This gives 6 elements of $S$ in total. Then, using the complementary principle of probability, that is straightforward, we will count the outcomes where no two of the lines sum to each other. Each element of sides $1,2,3,4,5$ create 6 outcomes for diagonals : $1-2, 3-4, 5-1, 2-1, 3-4, 4-3$. After algebra and simplification, we finally reach $$\mathrm{Pr}(1,s\cap 1)= \binom{6}{2}+3 \cdot 1\sum_{i=1}^5\binom{i}{2} \implies \mathrm{/R}\,\cdot\,\mathrm{/T}=\frac{12}{60}=\boxed{\frac{1}{5}}$$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m  GUIContent
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The lines of the pentagon $B^*A^*C^*D^*E^*$ with the same length:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $\quad A^*D^* \quad \; , \quad B^*A^* \quad \; , \quad C^*B^* \quad \; , \quad D^*C^* \quad \; , \quad E^*A^* \quad \; , \quad D^*E^* \quad \; , \quad E^*B^* \quad \; $ (which give $\binom{6}{2}$) and we are left, $D=C,B=E$, to choose the permutation $A=D,C=E,B=D$, by pick permutation $(C)$ has $\frac{{6 \choose 2}}{2!}$ possibilities. In this case we return with the quotient $-1$
