[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We use the slope-intercept form to find the equation of a line. Divide the $x$ term in the slope-intercept form by $-2$ to obtain its slope in the form $y=kx+b$. Then, plug in the given $x$-intercept value into the equation and solve for its $y$-intercept value. $	\\begin{array}{|l|l|}\\hline\hline y=-2x-5&5==-2(5)+b\\\\ 14&b=19\\hline\end{array}$ $\therefore$ The slope of the $y$-intercept line is $-2$ and its $y$-intercept point occurs at $(0,19)$.<|endoftext|>
