[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 	$1121_5 = 1 \cdot 5^3 + 1 \cdot 5^2 + 2 \cdot 5 + 1 \quad (\text{multiply with } 12_5 = 12)$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 	$12_5 = 1 \cdot 5 + 2 \quad (\text{multiply with } 10_5 = 10))$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 2. Divide the sum of these two numbers by the decimal (base $10$) value of the divisor $12_5 = 12$:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 	$1121_5 \div 12_5 = (1 \cdot 5^3 + 1 \cdot 5^2 + 2 \cdot 5 + 1 \cdot 5^2 + 1 \cdot 5)^{\text{popcount} \cdot 10 - 10} \equiv (($ $1 \cdot 5^3 + 1 \cdot 5^3 + 1 \cdot 5^3))^{\text{popcount} \cdot 10 - 10} \text{ (mod } 10)$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 3. Simplify the expression inside the square brackets:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 	$(1 \cdot 5^3 + 1 \cdot 5^3 + 1 \cdot 5^3) = 3 \cdot 5^3$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 4. Apply the formula for base $10$ division of several numbers modulo $n$:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 	we split out the last digit when computing the following quotient:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 	$(1 \cdot 5^3 + 1 \cdot 5^3 + 1 \cdot 5^3)^{\text{popcount} \cdot 10 - 10} \equiv 3 \cdot (5^3)^{\text{popcount} \cdot 10 - 10} \text{ (mod } 10)$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 5. Change the last digit for divisibility:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 	$3 \cdot (5^3)^{\text{popcount} \cdot 10 - 20} \equiv (5^3)^{\text{popcount} \cdot 10 - 10} \text{ (mod } 10)$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 6. Compute $5^3 = 125$:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 	$(5^3)^{\text{popcount} \cdot 10 - 20} \equiv 125^{\text{popcount} \cdot 10 - 10} \equiv 25^{\text{popcount} \cdot 10} \text{ (mod } 10)$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 7. When reaching $10$:
