[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The dimensions of a triangle are tripled to form a new triangle. If the area of the new triangle is 54 square feet, how many square feet were in the area of the original triangle?
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To find the area of the original triangle, we need to consider the formula for the area of a triangle, which is \( A = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height} \).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m First, let's determine the dimensions of the new triangle. The original triangle has sides of length 6, 8, and 10. When the dimensions are tripled, the new side lengths will be \(3 \times 6 = 18\), \(3 \times 8 = 24\), and \(3 \times 10 = 30\).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Next, we can find the area of the new triangle using these new side lengths. The base and height of the new triangle will be the same as the base and height of the original triangle when the bases and heights are scaled down by a factor of 3.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So, the base and height of the new triangle will be \(6 \times 3 = 18\) and \(8 \times 3 = 24\), respectively. The height of the new triangle will also be \(3 \times 3 = 9\) (since the height is halved when the base is halved).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The area of the new triangle is:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
