[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m When a class of math students lined up for a class picture on Picture Day, they found that when they stood in rows of four there was one person left over. When they formed rows of five students, there were two extra students, and when they lined up in rows of seven, there were three students left over. What is the fewest number of students possible in this class?
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m According to the given conditions, we can write two equations:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m N & = 4a + 1 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m N & = 5b - 2 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m N & = 7c - 3 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m where $N$ represents the number of students and $a, b, c$ represent the number of rows and the number of people per row, respectively. We should find integer solutions to these equations.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Taking the first equation modulo $4$, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m N & = 3a + 1 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Taking the first equation modulo $5$, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m N & = 2b - 2 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m and after substituting into the fourth one, we arrive at:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 3a + 1 & = 2b - 2 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m and then solving for $a$ and $b$, we find $a = 3$ and $b = 4$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Substituting back into the first equation, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m N & = 14 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Looking at the sequence $3a + 1 = 14$, we find that the pattern is $3, 4, 7, 10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 25, 28, 31, \ldots$.$\blacksquare$<|endoftext|>
