[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m determine the possible number of elements between \( x_0 \) and \( x_{99} \) that maintain this sequence as well.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To solve the problem of determining the possible number of elements between \( x_0 \) and \( x_{99} \) that maintain the sequence \( A_0, A_1, \ldots, A_{99} \) as given in the problem, we need to consider 6 distinct positive integers such that the sequence is linearly increasing from \( x_0 \) to \( x_{99} \). This means that:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ a_0 = 3, a_1 = 6, a_2 = 7, a_3 = 11, a_4 = 15 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Given that the sequence is linearly increasing, the possible values for \( x_i \) are the set of distinct integers that can form a sequence satisfying these constraints. We need to find how many of these integers are less than \( x_{99} \).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The following list can be used to find the number of valid \( x_i \) values:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \{11, 15, 19, 20, 23, 26, 29, 30, 33, 35, 39, 40, 43, 45, 49, 50, 53, 56, 59, 60, 62, 63, 66, 69, 70, 73, 75, 79, 80, 83, 85, 88, 90, 91, 93, 95, 98\} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
