[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The equation $x^2-4x+7=19$ has two solutions, $a$ and $b$, with $a\geq b$. What is the value of $2a+b$?
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The equation $x^2-4x+7=19$ is $x^{2}-4x-12=0$ . This is easy to factor since $x^2+6x+8$. Completing the square we get $x^2+6x+9-9=0$ . The (non-integer) roots are $x=3 \pm 3 \sqrt{3}$ . Putting $x=a$ and $x=b$ we get $a \geq b$ . $2a + b \geq 2a \geq 3 \sqrt{3} + 9$ or $2a + b = 9 + 3 \sqrt{3}$ . $\blacksquare$
