[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m If each fractal set has an initial point mass of 1, the function describes how the mass changes over time as the set expands. A fractal set is a self-similar set, which can be expressed as the union of smaller topological spaces. The topology of each topological space is based on the pattern of similar connections between points, much like the way scale structure in geology is implied by hypothesis.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m For this fractal, the first set will presumably repeat itself if you move to each spot on a square, and the same for every square. While we could include two dimensional fractals, this takes up much more space.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m In order to calculate the fractional area and volume/total area of these two dimensional shapes, we could first divide the area or volume of the geigers with revolutions by a total of 1. It would then be more accurate to take the square root of this number, even given the same assumptions. Keeping this in mind for this problem, we get: $$arctan\left(\frac 4\pi\right)=\operatorname{Ø-\frac 12the antiderivative of the first fractal set}.$$ $\operatorname{Ø-\frac 12}$ means there is arguably a $-\frac{1}{2}$ in there somewhere - think of it as binary representation of $-\frac{1}{2}$.  Proving it not too complicated.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Here is a link explaining why $Arctan(\frac 12)= -\frac{1}{2}$, which could be proven as a matter of self posterity from the Pythagorean theorem and using polar coordinates $\[\operatorname{Ø-\frac 12=a+(-\frac{\sqrt2}{2})^2=a+(\frac{\sqrt2}2)^2\]$. If we were to try putting that number into our problem for $x=3$, $\operatorname{Ø-\frac 12} x^2 \approx 0.3580847(1+0.8i)$. Here is another image by Andrew Wiles inspired from the one by KovnerEven if this is og request a covered by a scope, maybe it are just about a rabbit.
