[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The problem is asking for the area of the gray region bound by the circles and the x-axis.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To solve this, we can consider the circles as being inscribed by the ovals and find the areas under each oval and vertically above the x-axis.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We can use the equation of the circles and the area formula $A = 4\pi r^2$ to find the area of each oval and the vertical area above the x-axis.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The area of each circle is $4\pi(4^2) = 64\pi$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Since they are incided by the ovals, the area under each oval is $64\pi$. Therefore, the area under the ovals is $64\pi + 64\pi = 128\pi$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Finally, we can find the area of the gray region by subtracting the area of the vertical region from the area of the ovals.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The decimal placement will change when calculating the distance, so we rewrite it as:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$\text{Area} = 128\pi - 64 + 64 = \boxed{160\pi\text{ units}}.$$
