[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Let $f(x)$ be an even function, and let $g(x)$ be an odd function.  Is $f(x) g(x)$ even, odd, or neither?
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Enter "odd", "even", or "neither".
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To solve this problem, we need to use the definitions of even and odd functions.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m A function is even if $f(-x) = f(x)$ for all values of $x$ in its domain.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m A function is odd if $g(-x) = -g(x)$ for all values of $x$ in its domain.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Given that $f(x)$ is even and $g(x)$ is odd, we substitute the definitions into the given expression $f(x) g(x)$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So, $f(x) g(x) = f(-x) g(-x)$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Since $f(x)$ is even, $f(-x) = f(x)$. And since $g(x)$ is odd, $g(-x) = -g(x)$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Substituting these values, we get $f(x) g(x) = f(x) (-g(x)) = - f(x) g(x)$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m This shows that the function $f(x) g(x)$ is an odd function.
