[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The sign function $\text{sgn}(x)$ is defined by \[\text{sgn}(x) = \left\{ \begin{aligned} -1 \quad &\text{if } x < 0 \\ 0 \quad &\text{if } x = 0 \\ 1 \quad &\text{if } x > 0. \end{aligned} \right. \]Compute $\sum_{n=-10}^{20} \text{sgn}(n).$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Let's calculate $\sum_{n=-10}^{20} \text{sgn}(n)$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m First, we need to understand what the difference and quotient of the signs are. By observing how these signs pair together, we can see that they almost cancel each other out.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m When adding numbers that have the same sign, we add them to each other. When adding numbers with signs that differ by an odd multiple, we subtract one from the other.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m In this case, we have the full set of numbers -1 to 20. Since 20 is even, we'll get two pairs of numbers with the same sign (20, 19), 19, 18, and so on, and -10, -9, -8.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m For each pair, since their signs are the same (both odd multiples of $2)$, we can simply add them to each other: $19 + 18 + \ldots + (-2)$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Now, since the last term is always the negative of $-10$, we can remove it from the sum: $(19 + 18 + \ldots + (-2)) + (-10)$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Since there are $19+1 \times 20/2 = 96$ terms, and the signs average out, this sum will be $1857$.
