[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Sure, I'll work through this problem step by step. Since the problem asks for the maximum value of the function, I'll consider two extreme cases: when $n$ approaches different integer values and when $n = 10$ jumps through the function definition.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m ### Case 1: When $n$ approaches 10 from below
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m If $n < 10$, then based on the definition of $f(n)$, $f(n)$ becomes $f(n) = n + 10$. Therefore, as $n$ gets smaller and closer to 10, $f(n)$ increases without bound.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m ### Case 2: When $n = 10$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m When $n = 10$, according to the definition, $f(10) = f(10 - 5) = f(5)$. And since $5 \ge 10$, according to the definition, $f(5) = f(5 - 5) = f(0)$. Thus, regardless of how small things get, the output remains $f(0) = 0 + 10 = 10$. So dividing by 10 gives us our result, $f(10) = 10$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To ensure we find the largest possible value, we should check lower numbers than 10 going forward until it becomes less than 10:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - Start with $n < 5$: $f(n) = n + 10 = 15$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - Increase to $n = 1$: $f(n) = f(1 - 5) = f(-4) = -4 + 10 = 6$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - Adjust to higher values, like $n < 1$: $f(n) = n + 10 = 11$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Hence, taking an average means that this pattern seems increasing constantly: decreasing from 1 to 6 followed by increasing back to 11. But these average approaches increase at almost constant rate but technically do not reach the upper bound because eventually, you will reach or even surpass $n = 10$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Summarizing the travel speed graphically would be counter-intuitive.<|endoftext|>
