[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Yes, I can help you with that. Here's the solution:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \boxed{{}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Since A and B are complementary, we know that A + B = 90°. Therefore,
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \cos(A) + \sin(B) = \cos(90° - A) + \sin(A) = \sin(A) + \sin(A) = 2\sin(A)}(1)
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Since the sum of angles in a triangle is 180°, we also know that:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m AB = AC - BC
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So,
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \sqrt{50} = 4 - BC
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m BC = 4 - \sqrt{50}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m BC = \boxed{2}<|endoftext|>
