[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m When three standard dice are tossed, the numbers $a,b,c$ are obtained. Find the probability that $$(a-1)(b-1)(c-1) \neq 0$$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m There are $6^3=216$ possibilities for the combinations $(a,b,c)$, as each of the $6$ possible values of each individual dice is tossed $3$ times. First we note the number of combinations which satisfy $$(a-1)(b-1)(c-1) \neq 0.$$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $$\begin{align*}ac&=1,\\bc&=1,\\ab&=1\\bc&=2,\\bc&=4.\end{align*}$$ Note that $(ac)^2 + (bc)^2 = 1$, and $(ab)^2 + (bc)^2 = 1$, and $(ab)^2 + (ca)^2 = 5$, and $(ac)^2 + (ca)^2 = 5$.<br>We only need to work out the statistics for the numbers which yield a count of either $1$ or $-1$ or $4$: there are $3+3+4=10$ of these: - $(a,b,c) = (1,0,0)\rightarrow (1-1)(0-1)(0-1)=0$, - $(a,b,c) = (1,0,1)\rightarrow (1-1)(0-1)(1-1)=0$, - $(a,b,c) = (1,1,0)\rightarrow (1-1)(1-1)(0-1)=0$, - $(a,b,c) = (1,1,1)\rightarrow (1-1)(1-1)(1-1)=0$, - $(a,b,c) = (0,1,0)\rightarrow(0-1)(1-1)(0-1)=0$, - $(a,b,c) = (0,1,1)\rightarrow(0-1)(1-1).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \frac{125}{216}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
