[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 2001 = 3 \times 667
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We should check if \( 667 \) is a prime number. If it's not, then \( 3 \times 667 \) cannot be factored further as a product of distinct prime numbers. Now, let's factorize \( 667 \) further:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 667 = 7^2 \times 31
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m This factorization shows that \( 2001 = 7 \times 7 \times 31 \). Since \( I \) cannot be a multiple of 3 as it is a distinct integer, \( I \) must be a prime number (because prime numbers do not have any factors other than 1 and themselves).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So, let's try different values for \( I \), \( M \), and \( O \) while ensuring the product is \( 2001 \). We will start with some reasonable bounds for \( I \) and \( O \):
