[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To find the closest fraction to the fraction 1/15 of the 2007 Math Olympics medal winners, we start by identifying the fraction represented by 1/4: $\frac{1}{4}$. 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Next, we perform the repeated division of 1 to get the decimal form, which gives:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ 1 \div 4 = 0.25 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The decimal 0.25 can be converted to a fraction. First, multiply the decimal by 4:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ 0.25 \times 4 = 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m This tells us:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ 4 \times 0.25 = 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Thus,
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ 1 = \frac{4}{4} = \frac{1}{4} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We find that the closest fraction to \(\frac{1}{4}\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \boxed{\frac{1}{4}} \]<|endoftext|>
