[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Yes, you can certainly do that. I’ll break it down into steps as if you hadn’t seen it before:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 1. You’ve already written the expression for the quotient $\frac{a+x+b}{c}$. Given the given roots, we want to express it in terms of $x-\sqrt{2}$ and $x-\sqrt{3}$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 2. Replace $c$ with $(1+\sqrt{2})(1+\sqrt{3})$, which gives us:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ c=2+3\sqrt{5}E.g. \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 3. So, the expression becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \frac{(1+\sqrt{2}+ \sqrt{6}+ \sqrt{5})(2+3\sqrt{5})}{(1-\sqrt{2}+ \sqrt{6}+ \sqrt{5})(2+3\sqrt{5})} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 4. Simplify the numerator and the denominator separately. First, multiply the numerator and the denominator:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m &\frac{(1+\sqrt{2}+ \sqrt{6}+ \sqrt{5})}{(1-\sqrt{2}+ \sqrt{6}+ \sqrt{5})(2+3\sqrt{5})} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m &= \frac{(1+\sqrt{2}+ \sqrt{6}+ \sqrt{5})(2+3\sqrt{5})}{(2+3\sqrt{5})^2} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m &= \frac{(1+\sqrt{2}+ \sqrt{6}+ \sqrt{5})(2+3\sqrt{5})}{4+12\sqrt{10}+45} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m &= \frac{3(\sqrt{2}+1+\sqrt{6}+\sqrt{5})^2}{4+12\sqrt{10}+45} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m &= \frac{3(\sqrt{2}+1+\sqrt{6}+\sqrt{5})^2}{18} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m &= \frac{(\sqrt{2}+1+\sqrt{6}+\sqrt{5})(\sqrt{2}+1-\sqrt{6}-\sqrt{5})}{6}.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m E.g. KanV
