[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m First, let's calculate the total area of the circle with a 2 cm radius. The formula for the area of a circle is $\pi r^2$, where $r$ is the radius of the circle. Plugging in the radius of the circle, we get $\pi (2\text{cm})^2 = 4\pi \text{cm}^2$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Next, we need to calculate the area of the 25 squares. Each square measures 2 cm by 2 cm, so the area of each square is $2\text{cm} \times 2\text{cm} = 4 \text{cm}^2$. Since there are 25 squares, the total area of the squares is $25 \times 4\pi \text{cm}^2 = 100\pi \text{cm}^2$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Now, we need to find the area of the visible shaded region. The visible shaded region is the area of the circle minus the area of the 25 squares it covers, minus the area of the 5 circles the circles are placed on top of it. The area of the circle that is not covered by the 25 squares is the area of the circle minus the area of the 25 squares, which is $4\pi \text{cm}^2$. So, the area of the visible shaded region is $100\pi \text{cm}^2 - 4\pi \text{cm}^2 = 96\pi \text{cm}^2$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Finally, we need to find the value of $A+B$. Since the area of the visible shaded region is $96\pi \text{cm}^2$ and $A-2B \pi = 4\pi$, we can easily see that $96 = 2B$. So, $A = 54 \pi$ and $B = 7.2\pi$. Thus, $A+B = 54 + 7.2 = 61.2$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The answer is \boxed{61.2}.<|endoftext|>
