[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 2. From the second point on the edge, the fly walks straight to another point on the edge. But since it left the initial point on the edge to reach a later part of the circle, it's like trying to get travel a negligibly small distance compared to the number of times it steps on the edge. This means we're talking about a tiny ring of the drawing on the diagram. 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m All flights are within, so we can account for those points' measures. So the path length to the third point (circle edge, back) is twice the actual radius: \(58 \times 2 = 116\) feet.
