[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Five workers paint four houses in six days. Working at the same rate as these workers, how many workers are needed to paint 12 houses in three days?
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To determine how many workers are needed to paint 12 houses in three days, we first need to find out how many houses one worker can paint in one day.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The workers can paint four houses in six days. Therefore, one worker can paint:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \frac{4 \text{ houses}}{6 \text{ days}} \times 1 \text{ day/worker} = \frac{4}{6} \text{ houses/worker/day} = \frac{2}{3} \text{ houses/worker/day}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So, one worker can paint \(\frac{2}{3}\) houses per day.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Now, to find out how many workers are needed to paint 12 houses in three days, we multiply the number of workers one worker can paint per day by the number of days:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
