[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Three-fourths of the students in Mr. Shearer's class have brown hair and six-sevenths of his students are right-handed.  If Mr. Shearer's class has 28 students, what is the smallest possible number of students that could be both right-handed and have brown hair?
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Let's use variables to represent the unknown quantities and set up an equation to solve for them.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m If there are 28 students in total, and 'x' is the number of right-handed people who have brown hair, we know that 'x' is 3/4 of the students who have brown hair. So, 'x' is {eq}3/4\cdot 28 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m {/eq}.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Similarly, out of the 'x' students who have brown hair, '3/7' of them are right-handed. So, '3/7\cdot 28' is the number of right-handed people who have brown hair.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Let's calculate:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m {eq}\begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m x &= \frac{3}{4}\cdot 28 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 3x &= 3\cdot x &\left(\text{since } x = \frac{3}{4}\cdot 28\right) \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m x &= \frac{3}{4}\cdot 3\cdot 14 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m &= \frac{9}{1}\cdot 14 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m &= \boxed{126}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Therefore, the smallest possible number of students who could be both right-handed and have brown hair is 126.<|endoftext|>
