[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 2. Then, check the powers of 13 modulo 13:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    \[13^1 = 13\]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    \[13^2 = 169 \equiv 13 \mod 13 - 1 = 13 - 1 = 12 \mod 13\]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    \[13^3 = 13^3 = 2197 \equiv 12 \mod 13 - 1 = 12 - 1 = 11 \mod 13\]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    \[13^4 = 13^4 = 28561 \equiv 11 \mod 13 - 1 = 11 - 1 = 10 \mod 13\]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m   Continuing this process, we notice that:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m    \[13^k \equiv 11 \mod 13 \text{ for } k = 1, 2, 3, 4, \ldots\]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 3. Considering that $3^{1999}$ belongs to the set generated by powers of 13 modulo 13: 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 3^{1999} \equiv 3^{n} \mod 10^2
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m where $n = 1999$, since \(1999\) is indeed a single modulus, we are looking for the modulo \(13\) equivalent of \(3^n \mod 13\):
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
