[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We have to find out the value of sin(60°). Let's apply the fact for knowing\n <br />\n$\nsin x  = \sqrt{(cos^2 x) + (sin^2 x)}\ \ \ \ (1)\$<br />\normalsize\n<br />\n<\hskip-1cm>First, we calculate (cos^2 x + sin^2)x= <br />\n( frac{1}{{2}}){x}^2 + (frac{sqrt{3}}{2}{x}^3)<br />\normalsize and then \underline{sin x + frac{sqrt{3}sin x}{2}} <br />\nNow associate \underline {d(x)}\ \ into equation (1), to storcev.e a new value instead of x and we'll end up with <br />\n$\nsin 60^\circ = \sqrt{frac{1}{2}} + frac{sqrt{3}}{2},$<br />\nwhich will be reduced to $\frac{sqrt3}{2} + frac{sqrt3}{2},$ interpreting it that $\frac{2sqrt3}{2}$ is equivalent to $\frac{sqrt3}{2}\,\left(body\ under --------------- \frac{sqrt3}{2}\right)$<br />\nLetting our result - - $\frac{sqrt3}{2}\,\left(body\ under --------------- \frac{sqrt3}{2}\right) <br />\nNow one compare \sqrt be nine with \sqrt{3}, with \frac{sqrt3}{2} compared to \frac{sqrt3}{2}\,\left(body\ body charged under -------- \frac{sqrt3}{2}\right)\ \ under ------------------------ \frac{sqrt3}{2} + frac{sqrt3}{2} ,<br />\nIt will be concluded that the original be nine is simply one half, that equates to 45 degrees and its sine is simply <cmath>$\frac{sqrt3}{2} + frac{sqrt3}{2}$<cmath>. So finally, $\boxed{45\ degrees}$ is the be that equals sin.quent6b ti test.<|endoftext|>
