[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Sure! We need to find all pairs of numbers $(x, y)$ from $1$ to $99$ such that their digits sum up to $7$:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 1. For a two-digit integer, let’s denote $x$ as the tens digit and $y$ be the units digit.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 2. Each digit must be an integer between $1$ and $9$ (since they are two-digit numbers), inclusive.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So, we have the constraint:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[ x + y = 7 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Here's a step-by-step reasoning:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - By rewriting the equation, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m   ```display圖片
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m   y = 7 - 1 \\ [29 attachment]"
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m   x \\ [06 attachment]<<
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m   ```
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - Every possible value (integer from $1$ to $9$) can be used here.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 1. There are no restrictions on $x$, so it can be any integer from $1$ to $9$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 2. No restrictions on $y$, so similarly it can be any integer from $1$ to $9$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Hence, we arrive at the equation of numbers $(x, y)$ that sum to the number $7$ within the range $1$ through $99$:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \boxed{ (7 \text{ and } y), \ (y \text{ and } 7-y), \ (6, 1, 4, 8) \, \text{above} y \, \text{and} \, (5, 3, 9) \, \text{below} y} */
