[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Thessaly and Cretian agricultural areas had a much higher level of levels of protection than the rest of Greece, yet despite improvements in farm management techniques that were attributed to Thessaly, Cretian farmers continued to experience similar conditions as their Greek neighbors. In large part because of their struggles to protect their homesteads from external forces, the Cretian people did not establish the agricultural regions that these Thessaly people established, (Schreiber 1989). Not only did Cretian farmers continue to struggle in the interwar period of war, but established relationships forming themselves within the agricultural areas, who would have important or even obligatory duties in these rural areas. As such, this chapter examines agriculture as a manner of social and political cohesion in the historical time period where the kingdom flourished, from the initiation of women into literary development until the 16th century (Folano 1990).
