[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To find the day of the week for the first day of the same month with a Friday the Thirteenth, we need to consider the cyclical nature of the week and the distribution of Fridays in a month.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m First, we identify the specific pattern. Fridays appear twice as often as other days within month A/Boom advent calendar (Monday to Saturday). So, if we add a day to the first of March (which would be 21st April), and the remaining days of the week realistically, it would be 22nd March.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Here's the reasoning:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m April has 30 days with the first of March as our starting point. Since a Friday is also a symbol of the first time in a new month, we can approximate that the first day of the month will have the same day of the week. Therefore, if the start day is March 21st, the start of the month will be March 22nd, and the first day of the same month will be March 22nd.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Finally, since a week is 7 days, we will divide the total week count by 7 to wrap around to the first day of the next week.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Thus:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - March 21st + (7 - 2) days = March 22nd
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - 20th of March (total = 7 for 7 days) end – March 22nd end = March 22nd if start day of March is 21st (single day)
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - For this step, please ensure the 7 alternative weekdays are in range.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
