[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The admission fee for an exhibition is $ \$25$ per adult and $ \$12$ per child.  Last Tuesday, the exhibition collected $ \$1950$ in admission fees from at least one adult and at least one child.  Of all the possible ratios of adults to children at the exhibition last Tuesday, which one is closest to $ 1$?
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Let $ A$ be the set of adults and $ C$ be the set of children.  By the problem, ${A,C}$ has $ \binom {3}{2}$  ordered pairs. Now the first condition gives us $ 25 A + 12 C$  and the second gives us $ 25 A + 15 C = 1950$ .  Equate the two expressions for $ 25 A + 15 C = 1950$  to get $ A + 3 C = 62$ . Set $ C = x$  and equate the two equations only for positive $ \color{lightblue} c$ . We get ${3x + 62 - 25x = 1950 = 19x + 62$ so $ x$  can take $ 12,14,16$ and $ 18$  respectively (check the range where the sets are.) In conclusion, $ \dfrac{3}{19}=1.42$ , $ \dfrac{3}{55}=0.057$ , $ \dfrac{2}{14}=0.143$ and $ \dfrac{5}{18}=0.278$ . The ratio closest to, say 1, is $\boxed{{0.278}}$ . endorsersystem
