[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We'll first add the areas of the lower triangular lobe and the top triangular lobe. The lower triangular lobe has a base of 3 cm and a height of 4 cm, so its area is $\frac{1}{2}\cdot 3\cdot 4 = 6$ cm$^2$. Similarly the top triangular lobe has a base of 4 cm and a height of 3 cm, so its area is $\frac{1}{2}\cdot 4\cdot 3 = 6$ cm$^2$. 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The volume of the smaller solid that results is the difference of the areas of the lobs, $\frac{1}{2}\cdot 3\cdot 4 - \frac{1}{2}\cdot 4\cdot 3 = 6 - 6 = 0$ cm$^3$. 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Therefore, there is no way for the volume of the resulting solid to be greater than 0 cm$^3$.
