[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m A circular ceiling fan rotates at a constant speed of $80$ revolutions per minute.  A point halfway between the center of the fan and the outer edge of the fan travels $97968$ inches in $15$ minutes.  How far (in inches) does a point on the outer edge of the fan travel in $30$ minutes?
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To find the distance a point on the outer edge of the fan travels in 30 minutes, we first need to determine the circumference of the circle the fan makes. Since the fan rotates at a constant speed of 80 revolutions per minute, we can calculate the distance traveled in one revolution as $3600$ inches (since one revolution involves turning $360$ degrees).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Next, we need to find the distance traveled in one minute:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \frac{97968 \text{ inches}}{15 \text{ minutes}}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Now, let's calculate the circumference in inches per minute:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \frac{97968}{15} = 6597.8 \text{ inches/minute}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Finally, to find the distance traveled in 30 minutes, we multiply the circumference by 30:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 6597.8 \text{ inches/minute} \times 30 \text{ minutes} = 197934 \text{ inches}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So, a point on the outer edge of the fan travels \(\boxed{197934}\) inches in 30 minutes.<|endoftext|>
