[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Mr. Cole needs to select 3 girls and 5 boys to go on a backpacking trip. In how many ways can he do this if 6 girls and 11 boys are interested in going on the trip?
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To solve this problem, we can use the concept of combinations, also known as "n choose k" or $binom{n}{k}$, which represents the number of ways to choose a certain number of items from a larger set without regard to order.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Let's break it down step-by-step:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We have 11 boys interested in going on the trip.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m When we choose 3 girls to go along with them, there are a total amount of ways to choose Boys and Girls at a time. So $$\binom{11}{3}$$ is the number of possible groups made from the boys and the girls. This can be simplified to: $$ = \dfrac{ 11! }{ 6! \cdot 5! } $$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Given that there will always be 6 girls to choose from, we don't have any way to choose more than 3 out of the 11 girls if there are only 6 left. Therefore the answer may be 6 instead of the given 13.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m We can divide the groups of choosing 3 girls from the boys, and choosing 5 subjects from 11 subjects.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The answer may vary, as stated above;
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 1Person 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 1 Girl
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 5Person
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 5 Button 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 3Girl
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Due to some overlap in the selection of groups of 3 girls and 5 students, using the binom function can result in an incorrect answer.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m If we choose 3 girls out of 6 and 5 students out of 11 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The possibility of doing this correction:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $\dbinom{6}{3} \times \dbinom{11}{5} = \dfrac{6!}{3! \times (6-3)!} \times \dfrac{11!}{5! \times (11-5)!}$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Cancel 5! from the numerator and denominator:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $6! \div 6! \times 6 \times 11! \div 5! \times 11!=6/5 \times11!$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m The answer ends up being $\boxed{221}$<|endoftext|>
