[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To find the integer \( n \) that satisfies \( -200 \equiv n \pmod{19} \), we need to find the remainder when \( -200 \) is divided by \( 19 \). This can be done by performing the following calculation:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m First, we find the quotient and remainder when \( 200 \) is divided by \( 19 \):
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 200 \div 19 = 10 \text{ remainder } 10
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m This means that:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 200 = 19 \cdot 10 + 10
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Thus,
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 200 \equiv 10 \pmod{19}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Since \( -200 = -10 \), we can substitute this value back into our original equation:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m -200 \equiv -10 \equiv 10 \pmod{19}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Therefore, the integer \( n \) that satisfies \( -200 \equiv n \pmod{19} \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \boxed{10}
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
