[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Note that any four-digit odd number ending in odd digits is odd, so all results are $4$ or $5$.  If $B$ is $0$ or $1$, then the number is $0$ or $1$.  If $B$ is $2$: it has to have at least one of the things that are two digits apart, so it is either a three-digit number or a four-digit number.  It needs to be $3$, $4$ or $5$, so that leaves the $B$ equal to $7$.  In either case, the number has to be a four-digit number with two digits below one $0$ or $1$, leading to a minimum base of $9$.  Otherwise, getting $9$ is simply infeasible.  If $B$ is $8$, it must have $9$, so is $9$, so it can be a maximum with the clue of an even number below it to avoid the cases of $9$ and $1$.  Otherwise, it may also be minimum or maximum.  A greedy method would be to consider the number and do the same thing for all of the digits until you tried them all, then just find the maximum ($B$ is $3$) or the minimum ($B$ is $0$ as were $37$ and $39$ above).  We can optimally begin here with a case where $B\not = 0$, since there are only four cases of nonzero $B$, $D$, such that we are guaranteed the minimum for that criterion.  If $B$ is $0$, we can choose any even digits, and it quickly becomes clear that $B = 5005$ is the minimum of $8$, or maximally, $5000$.  To see what a minimum number for $B$ could possibly be with an even $B$, we create a number with various $B$ integers, and see if a minimum number of digits in $B$ exists without other digit containing too many odd numbers.  For $B = 0$, this sequence is seen to have $9$, and never go higher.  For $B = 1$, as an example, $B$ has the greatest number of $odds$ in relation to $5000$, namely $0$, but we see that $50-14+5=29$ is the closest we could get without going past $B = 7$. <|endoftext|>
