[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m To solve the problem, we need to use the properties of similar triangles since lines \( k \) and \( \ell \) are parallel. The key property here is that corresponding angles of parallel lines are congruent, and angle bisectors split corresponding angles into equal segments. Therefore, angles in the opposite segments to \( x \) on lines \( k \) and \( \ell \) are equal to angle \( x \).
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m - Since lines \( k \) and \( \ell \) are parallel, 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m   \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m   \angle x \quad \text{(opposite angle for direction where the parallel lines cross)} =
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m   \angle B
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m   \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Thus, we can solve the angle as simply:
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \angle x = \angle A
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m So:
