[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m $6x^2 + x - 2 < 0$ then $x < 2/6\\Bigg(1+x)$
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m Also $5 < 2/6\\Bigg(1+x)$ is not true ... if we keep $2/6 \\Bigg(1+x)$² on the left and $1+x$ on the right side of the fence...The minimum value of $x$ is therefore $\frac{5}{(2/6)^{3/2}}=\frac{225}{16}$.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m InlineData of a cubic
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m This is slightly more difficult. We want a segment with endpoints $(3-2\sqrt5,5)$ and $(3+2\sqrt5,17)$ but with radii 1 unit. Let $A$ and $B$ be the foci of a cubic with vertex $(1,2)$, and let $H$ denote the intersection of $AB$ and the $y$ axis.
[36m(main_task pid=2119687)[0m For the cube to have intersecting maximum segments with the axis, one third of the way around the edges of the cube must be intersection. And, as mentioned before, it should be common directrix, not if that this segment was taken in the left half of the cube. As discussed in the post above, the radius cutting along the $y$ axis must be relevant!<|endoftext|>
